{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Summary\n\nCalling the long-running certified gate op `review_plan` (and, by the same mechanism, `verify_completion`) through the **rebar MCP server** — the path AGENTS.md mandates for gate ops — returns an opaque transport error (`McpError -32001: Request timed out`) once the operation runs long enough to reach its LLM passes. The server keeps running and eventually signs the attestation, but the **client is given no completion signal and no job handle**, so from the caller's side the ticket is left in an *unknown state*.\n\n## Why this is higher-severity than \"observability\"\n\n1. **Unknown ticket/gate state.** The caller cannot tell \"still running\" from \"failed\" from \"succeeded\"; the verdict (PASS/BLOCK/attestation) is lost to the caller even though it exists server-side.\n2. **Paid-API waste from re-runs.** The natural response to `-32001` is to re-invoke `review_plan`; if the first run is still in flight, the re-invocation starts a SECOND billable multi-call LLM review, multiplying cost per ticket under retry loops.\n3. **Mandated path.** Agents are directed to route gate ops through MCP; the local-CLI recovery (`review-plan --status`) is a carve-out an MCP-only client (a hosted agent with no checkout) cannot reach.\n\n## Reproduce / actual / expected\n\nRepro (observed 2026-08-30, ticket 71b5-1ceb-131a-422d):\n1. `review_plan` over MCP on a plan that passes the deterministic P1–P11 floor (so it proceeds to the LLM four-pass, ~15–20 min).\n2. Actual: client receives `McpError -32001: Request timed out`. Server completes and signs; `rebar review-plan <id> --status` (local) later shows a signed op-cert, confirming the review PASSED and no work was lost.\n3. Expected: the caller receives either the verdict, or a durable **job handle / in-progress token** it can poll — and a re-invocation while a review is in flight must NOT start a second billable run.\n\n## Root cause (proven — Q1 spike)\n\nThe `-32001` is a **client-side** SDK request timeout while rebar's server runs the gate under `anyio.fail_after(None)` — so the server COMPLETES and signs after the client has given up, and the durable verdict already exists server-side (keepalive alone cannot be the primary fix). The app-level answer reuses the already-shipped `run_workflow` pattern: a `{run_id,status:'running'}` handle returned in ms on a background daemon thread, persisted to the git-backed event log, polled by a no-LLM durable-state read. Full SDK-level analysis: see the description-trim comment.\n\n## Approved remediation — 2026-08-30\n\nTwo-phase app-level fix reusing the shipped `run_workflow` pattern. No MCP `tasks` wire extension, no new dependency, no client-capability negotiation. Design resolved 2026-08-30 (see DESIGN RESOLVED comment).\n\n- **Phase 1 (own commit, lands FIRST — the immediate cost-stop, no wire change):** a new leaf module `src/rebar/_mcp_inflight.py` — an in-process singleflight registry (a second concurrent caller for the same key awaits the SAME computation and shares its verdict). Dedup key = `sha256` over `[gate_type, canonicalised ticket_id, resolved 40-hex basis SHA, variant, readonly]`; `force=True` bypasses. Purge-on-completion + a defensive max-age sweep. Default-ON, kill-switch env `REBAR_MCP_DEDUP=0`. The sync `review_plan`/`verify_completion` bodies in `_mcp_llm.py` are wrapped by it (async def + `anyio.to_thread`).\n- **Phase 2 (stacks on Phase 1):** additive async surface — `review_plan_start` / `verify_completion_start` (return a `{job_id,…,status:'running'}` handle in ms on a background daemon thread like `run_workflow`; verdict persists to the durable event log, surviving a blue-green flip). Poll via `plan_review_status` plus a new `verify_completion_status` (or a generic `gate_status`). A git-ignored `.rebar/gate_runs/<job_id>` index (mirroring `.rebar/workflow_runs/`) maps job_id→(ticket,gate,basis). Sync tools stay as a dedup-protected fallback. AGENTS.md + docstrings + docs steer agents to `*_start`+poll for long ops.\n\n**Poll status contract.** The `*_start` handle resolves via `gate_status` / `plan_review_status` / `verify_completion_status` to a **six-value** `status` that docs + tool docstrings enumerate in full: `running` (daemon alive — keep polling); `passed`/`failed` (terminal verdict; carry `durable` currency so the caller can confirm it persisted); `stale-running` (index reads running but no daemon past the grace window — a crashed leader; safe to re-`*_start`); `attaching` (**non-terminal** — job in flight but its index record has not landed; a follower `*_start` shares the leader's `job_id` and writes no index entry, so keep polling, never treat as missing); `unknown` (no index record and the registry does not know the `job_id` — re-`*_start`). The `attaching` vs `unknown` split is load-bearing: an MCP-only agent coded to the contract must not mis-treat an in-flight follower poll as a terminal/missing result. Per-state semantics detail: see the description-trim comment.\n\nDurability across a blue-green flip comes from the git-backed verdict (auto-committed+pushed), not in-process state (`stateless_http=True`). A flip that kills an in-flight run costs at most one re-run; a `run_finished` marker in a `finally` + a `stale-running` read bound the worst case.\n\n## Fix plan — file impact\n\nPhase 1 (dedup): new `src/rebar/_mcp_inflight.py`; `src/rebar/_mcp_llm.py` (wrap sync bodies); `src/rebar/mcp_server.py` (register); `docs/env-vars.md` (`REBAR_MCP_DEDUP`); tests `test_mcp_inflight.py` + `test_mcp_llm_dedup.py`. Phase 2 (async start): `src/rebar/_mcp_llm.py` (`review_plan_start`/`verify_completion_start` via `_register_gate_start_tools`); `src/rebar/_mcp_reads.py` (`verify_completion_status`/`gate_status`); `src/rebar/_mcp_models.py` (output models); `src/rebar/llm/gate_runs.py` + `src/rebar/llm/__init__.py` (durable poll-status resolution); `docs/mcp-reference.md` + `docs/plan-review-gate.md`; test `test_mcp_gate_async.py`. Respect module-size + the complexity ratchet (extract along seams; never bump the baseline).\n\nTests are deterministic and token-free: monkeypatch `rebar.llm.review_plan`/`verify_completion` with a fake that blocks on a `threading.Event`; two concurrent same-key calls ⇒ fake called exactly once, both get the same verdict (AC #2 proof); different `basis_ref` ⇒ two calls; post-completion re-call re-invokes; async-start returns a handle <100ms and poll transitions running→passed/failed; a fresh registry (simulated new container) still resolves the durable verdict.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [x] `review_plan`/`verify_completion` over MCP no longer leaves the caller with an opaque transport timeout and no state: the caller receives either the verdict or a pollable in-progress signal/handle.\n- [x] A re-invocation while a review for the same ticket+basis is in flight attaches to that run rather than starting a second billable LLM review (in-flight de-duplication). A test proves no second run is launched.\n- [x] The chosen contract (start+poll) is documented for MCP-only clients in docs/mcp-reference.md / docs/plan-review-gate.md.\n- [x] Portable: the mechanism does not depend on the local checkout being present (an MCP-only client can observe completion). In-process MCP-server logic over the git-backed store — no CI-provider coupling; satisfies project.portability.\n- [x] The poll status contract documents all six states (`running`/`passed`/`failed`/`stale-running`/`attaching`/`unknown`) in docs + tool docstrings, so an agent never mis-treats the non-terminal `attaching` follower poll as a terminal/missing result.\n\n## Attribution\n\nTo be recorded on close via `--caused-by`: the introducing change is whichever work exposed `review_plan`/`verify_completion` as synchronous MCP tools without an in-flight/progress contract (determine from `git log`/blame on `_mcp_llm.py`'s tool registration). Owner domain: MCP. Likely `--class=plan_defect` (the tool was shipped sync without an in-flight contract) — confirm during implementation."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1788218841991199001,"uuid":"ebc04859-cd10-4be1-b8a3-5785609761d9"}